<xml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
<comment id="note00" x="40" y="40" w="640" h="470">SUNBURN DEVICE  -  micro:bit firmware 3.1

How to read this workspace:
The program is laid out in 9 numbered sections in two rows, left to right. Each section starts with a note like this one.
Sections 1 and 2 are the whole program: 1 is the settings and start-up, 2 is the flowchart. Sections 3 to 9 are the parts the flowchart calls.

 1 Start here: settings      6 Sound and servo
 2 The flowchart             7 Bluetooth and serial
 3 Buttons                   8 Helpers
 4 Alerts                    9 OLED screen driver (optional)
 5 Screen and LEDs

Pins: UV sensor P1, servo P2, buzzer P0 (optional), OLED on I2C P19/P20 (optional).
Every function block has a comment: click the small "?" icon on a block to read what it does.
Tip: do not use "Format code" or "Clean up blocks" from the workspace menu, that throws this layout away.</comment><comment id="note01" x="900" y="40" w="730" h="176">1. START HERE: SETTINGS
The "on start" block. First every setting the program uses (sensor type, servo,
screen, UV bands, timings), then the start-up steps: this is "Device turns on" on
the flowchart. Change your hardware and timings here and nowhere else.</comment><comment id="note02" x="1850" y="40" w="5935" h="176">2. THE FLOWCHART
"forever" runs runFlowchart again and again. runFlowchart is the whole flowchart from top to bottom: read
the UV sensor, sensor in range?, phone sending online UV?, UV index?, then one of the four branches, wait
5 minutes, reapply timer expired?. Every box and decision is a function here with the same name, in the
same order as the flowchart. Read them top to bottom, column by column.</comment><comment id="note03" x="8005" y="40" w="480" h="176">3. BUTTONS
A = done (sunscreen is on / going inside). A also skips
a message scrolling on the LEDs, and while waiting it
shows the UV index and the countdown. B = wake from
standby. A+B = device turns off, or on again.</comment><comment id="note04" x="8705" y="40" w="1956" h="176">4. ALERTS
Flash and beep, tap the arm with the servo, and standby. Each one keeps going until A is pressed (or the
app sends "ack"), the device is turned off, or its time limit runs out. No answer within the time limit
means the device is probably not being worn, so it goes to standby until a button is pressed.</comment><comment id="note05" x="10881" y="40" w="2298" h="176">5. SCREEN AND LEDS
What the 5x5 LEDs show, and what the optional OLED screen shows: the UV number, the band, messages and
the countdowns. Messages scroll across the LEDs when there is no OLED.</comment><comment id="note06" x="40" y="3063" w="485" h="220">6. SOUND AND SERVO
Beeps on the V2 speaker (or a buzzer on P0), and the
servo on P2 whose arm taps the wrist.</comment><comment id="note07" x="745" y="3063" w="3683" h="220">7. BLUETOOTH AND SERIAL
Talks to the phone app (Bluetooth UART) and to the USB serial console with the same text lines.
Out every 2 s:  st=wait;uv=7.3;sen=7.1;onl=6.5;band=vhigh;spf=5400;spfn=3;fw=3.2;demo=0;pv=0.7;ck=183
Out on events:  ev=on  ev=off  ev=sunscreen  ev=reapply  ev=inside  ev=standby  ev=error
In (one per line):  uv=6.5   ack   zero   cal=7   demo=0/1/2   power=0   debug=1   vol=180   screen=1
ping   read
Every line out ends with ;ck=N (character codes added up, mod 256) so the app can drop a damaged line</comment><comment id="note08" x="4648" y="3063" w="3122" h="220">8. HELPERS
Small tools used by the rest: sensor presets, the timings, sorting, number and time formatting, tidying
up commands, the line checksum, and the debug lines (send debug=1 to see them).</comment><comment id="note09" x="7990" y="3063" w="6146" h="220">9. OLED SCREEN DRIVER (optional)
Only used if you add a 128x64 I2C screen (SSD1306 or SH1106) on P19/P20. Draws text on it without needing
an extension. The program detects the screen at start-up and works exactly the same without one. You
should not need to change anything here.</comment><block id="xRRgvHNlG#rZ^u`HECiY" type="pxt-on-start" x="900" y="266">
